I would go a step further:  the majority of *useful*
items in the dungeon comes from monster inventory.

Note that majority of useful items is not the same
thing as the _most useful_ items.  The most useful
item in the game is the wand of wishing, which in
most games is only found where guaranteed, at
the castle.  The second-most-useful item in the
game is probably a magic marker, which is usually
from either wishing or bones or occasionally a
result of random object generation.

But if we're counting the _number_ of useful
items, the lion's share of them are monster
starting inventory.  Potions alone -- all those
potions of healing, extra healing, full healing,
and gain level that the monsters are carrying,
plus all the ones you can #dip for holy water
(not least, the object detection potions you
get from almost every nymph you kill) --
generally add up to more than a hundred
genuinely useful items in most games by
the time I'm ready to wake the wizard.
Do wands and scrolls of teleport and create
monster count as useful?  Because you
get a trainload of those.  (Wands of striking
are also abundant, but I only consider them
to be useful in the early game, unless you
count polyfodder as a use.  Wands of
digging are more arguable: some of them
are useful, but I've never found a use for
more than about a third of them.)


